Limits are part of the storage format. Raising one later is safe and lowering one is not, so these start low. The benchmark story confirms or raises them.

| Limit | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Title | 500 bytes |
| Type name, part type name, relationship name | 100 bytes each |
| Body format label [Proposed] | 16 bytes |
| One part's position [Proposed] | 256 bytes |
| All properties of one relic | 64 KiB |
| One part's fields | 16 KiB |
| One part's body | 1 MiB |
| One link's pointer | 512 bytes |
| One schema version | 64 KiB |
| Live parts per relic | 2,000 |
| Links per relic | 2,000 |
| All current bodies of one relic together | 16 MiB |
| Schema versions per type | 500 |

- **The size of one item** (the first nine rows) is checked when it is saved and again when it is read, as task fields are today.
- **Counts and totals** (the last four rows) are checked only when something is being added, never when a relic is read. Two people adding at the same moment can pass a count without either doing anything wrong, and the relic must stay readable.
- At these numbers the part list comes to about 1.1 MiB at most and the links to about 1.6 MiB, both under the 4 MiB ceiling on a single object.
